exports[`built-in prompts > are unchanged (full brief) 1`] = `
{
  "classification": "You are a content classification expert.
Analyze the article and assign exactly one category and a small set of relevant tags.

Classification rules:
- Return valid JSON only.
- Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown fences.
- Do not add explanations outside the JSON.
- category must be exactly one of the availableCategories provided below.
- Do not invent a category.
- tags must be an array with at most 4 items.
- Tags must be specific and relevant to the article.
- Avoid duplicate tags.
- Avoid overly generic tags such as 'blog', 'article', 'technology', 'news' unless they are truly central.
- Prefer tags from availableTags when relevant, but you may return new tags if needed.

Return this exact JSON structure:
{
  "category": "One category from availableCategories",
  "tags": [
    "Specific tag 1",
    "Specific tag 2"
  ]
}

Available Categories:
1. News
2. Guides

Available Tags:
1. ai

Article Text:
Text",
  "editor": "You are an experienced editor.
Improve the text for readability, tone consistency, and clarity.
Remove redundancies and break up overly long sentences.
The article is written in Italian (it); keep it in Italian (it).

Brief:
- Audience: backend engineers
- Tone: dry
- Target keyword: caching
- Secondary keywords: ttl, cdn


Output rules:
- Return valid JSON only.
- Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown fences.
- Do not add explanations outside the JSON.
- The \`editedText\` field may contain Markdown content.
- Preserve useful Markdown formatting such as headings, lists, quotes, and code blocks.
- Escape all double quotes inside JSON string values.

Return this exact JSON structure:
{
  "editedText": "The edited article text in Markdown.",
  "changesSummary": "Short summary of the changes made."
}

Original text:
Raw text",
  "planner": "You are a pragmatic content strategist.
Create a simple and practical article outline for the topic: Caching
Write the section titles in Italian (it).

Brief:
- Audience: backend engineers
- Tone: dry
- Target keyword: caching
- Secondary keywords: ttl, cdn
- Editorial notes: No hype.

Return valid JSON only.
Use this exact shape: ["Section title", "Section title"]
Create 4 to 6 sections maximum.
Avoid overly broad, academic, or redundant sections.
The outline should be suitable for a clear blog post, not a long-form essay.
Include an introduction and a conclusion through the section titles.",
  "review": "You are a strict editorial reviewer for a blog.
Score the article below from 0 to 10 on each dimension. Use the whole range: 5 is mediocre, 8 is good, 10 is excellent.

Dimensions:
- seo: the title and meta description fit the content and are specific; keywords are used naturally.
- readability: clear sentences, consistent tone, easy to follow.
- structure: a logical flow with an introduction, well-ordered sections and a conclusion.
- completeness: every outline section is covered with enough substance.
- repetition: 10 means no repeated ideas or phrases, 0 means the text repeats itself heavily.

Output rules:
- Return valid JSON only.
- Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown fences.
- Scores are numbers between 0 and 10.
- \`rationale\` is two or three sentences naming the main weaknesses (or why it is good).

Return this exact JSON structure:
{
  "scores": {
    "seo": 0,
    "readability": 0,
    "structure": 0,
    "completeness": 0,
    "repetition": 0
  },
  "rationale": "Short explanation of the scores."
}

Title: A title
Meta description: A description

Outline:
- Why caching matters
- Invalidation
- Conclusion

Article:
Text",
  "seo": "You are an SEO expert.
Analyze the article content and produce SEO-optimized metadata.
The article is written in Italian (it): write the title, metaDescription, h1 and heading texts in Italian (it).

Brief:
- Audience: backend engineers
- Target keyword: caching
- Secondary keywords: ttl, cdn


Output rules:
- Return valid JSON only.
- Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown fences.
- Do not add explanations outside the JSON.
- The title must be at most 60 characters.
- The metaDescription must be at most 155 characters.
- The slug must be lowercase ASCII (a-z, 0-9 and hyphens only) whatever the language: drop accents and transliterate special characters.
- The h1 is only a suggested WordPress title, not article content.
- headings must contain only H2 and H3 items found or suggested from the article structure.

Return this exact JSON structure:
{
  "title": "SEO title, max 60 characters",
  "metaDescription": "SEO meta description, max 155 characters",
  "slug": "url-safe-slug",
  "h1": "Suggested WordPress title",
  "headings": [
    {
      "level": "H2",
      "text": "Section heading"
    }
  ]
}

Article Outline:
1. Why caching matters
2. Invalidation
3. Conclusion

Article Text:
Text",
  "writer": "You are a clear and concise technical blog writer.
Write a Markdown article about: Caching
Requirements:
- Do not include an H1 title.
- Start directly with the introduction.
- Use H2 sections aligned with the outline.
- Keep the article between 500 and 900 words.
- Write in a clear, practical, human tone.
- Prefer short paragraphs and simple explanations.
- Avoid unnecessary depth, filler, repetition, and academic style.
- If the topic is technical, explain only what is useful to the reader.
- End with a short practical conclusion.
- Write the whole article in Italian (it), including headings. Keep code, commands and proper names as they are.

Brief:
- Audience: backend engineers
- Tone: dry
- Target keyword: caching
- Secondary keywords: ttl, cdn
- Editorial notes: No hype.

Outline:
1. Why caching matters
2. Invalidation
3. Conclusion

A human reviewer read the previous version of this article and asks for changes. Write the whole article again and apply every change below:
shorter intro

Previous version:
Old text

Your previous attempt was rejected by automatic checks. Write the article again and fix every problem below:
fix the length",
}
`;

